==English==
{{was wotd|2012|July|24}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|sagacité}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|sagācitās||sagaciousness}}, from {{m|la|sagāx||of quick perception, acute, sagacious}}, from {{m|la|sāgiō||I perceive by the senses}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/səˈɡæsəti/|/səˈɡæsɪti/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|sagacities}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[keen|Keen]] [[sense]] of [[smell]].
#* '''1607''', {{w|Edward Topsell}}, ''The History of Four-footed Beasts, Serpents, and Insects'', London: G. Sawbridge et al., 1658, p. 352,<sup>[http://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100599721]</sup>
#*: {{...}} this Beast [the ''[[w:Egyptian mongoose|Ichneumon]]''] is not only enemy to the Crocodile and Asp, but also to their Egs, which she hunteth out by the '''sagacity''' of her nose, and so destroyeth them {{...}}
# The quality of being [[sage]], [[wise]], or able to make good decisions; the quality of being [[perceptive]], [[astute]] or [[insightful]].
#* '''1813''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ''{{w|Pride and Prejudice}}'', Volume 3, Chapter 15,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/42671/42671-h/42671-h.htm]</sup>
#*: Young ladies have great penetration in such matters as these; but I think I may defy even ''your'' '''sagacity''', to discover the name of your admirer.
#* '''1904''', {{w|M. P. Shiel}}, ''The Evil That Men Do'', London: Ward, Lock & Co., Chapter ,<sup>[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks14/1402541h.html]</sup>
#*: Immediately after the meal, when he was alone again, he set to work to examine Drayton’s papers, of which there lay quite a mass on the table near him and, leaning toward the lamp on his elbow, he weighed the meaning of each with a certain sideward '''sagacity''' of gaze, a '''sagacity''' that smiled in its self-sureness.
#*: Swiss Family Robinson- "....near the mouth of a creek, towards which all our geese and ducks betook themselves; and I, relying on their '''sagacity''', followed in the same course."

